How does Oracle RAC manage and distribute redo log entries across instances?
Oracle RAC uses Cache Fusion to efficiently distribute redo log entries across instances. This mechanism enables direct sharing and exchange of modified data blocks in the buffer cache, minimizing disk I/O. Redo log entries generated by one instance are transmitted and applied to relevant blocks on other instances, ensuring data consistency and synchronization across the cluster.

How does Oracle RAC handle data consistency during write operations, and what is the role of the Global Cache Service (GCS)?
Oracle RAC ensures data consistency using Cache Fusion and Global Cache Service (GCS). GCS tracks block ownership, manages locks (Exclusive & Shared), and coordinates inter-instance block transfers via Cache Fusion, reducing disk I/O. Past Image (PI) blocks ensure read consistency, while Redo and Undo maintain durability. GCS ensures cache coherency, lock management, and high performance in a multi-node RAC setup.

